# The newcomer's classic: a counter made without `var`, then given a new
# value from itself inside a loop and an `if`. Lunch 2 says a name without
# `var` may make a new value from the old one "on the next lines of the same
# block"; this is not the same block, so it is refused, with the message
# the Names reference gives for a nested block.
# Run with `lume check corpus/m120/a09_tea_count_fails.lume`
# Meant to FAIL.
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
# error: `count` is declared on line 18, outside this block; a new `count` here would hide it and be lost when the block ends
#   --> corpus/m120/a09_tea_count_fails.lume:21:7
#    |
# 21 |       count = count + 1
#    |       ^
#   help: declare it `var count` on line 18

def main:
  orders = ["tea", "cake", "tea", "soup"]
  count = 0
  for o in orders:
    if o == "tea":
      count = count + 1
  puts "#{count} teas"
